The [Epoch Capabilities Index](https://epoch.ai/benchmarks/eci) (ECI) puts 260 AI models on a single scale. What differences in that number ultimately mean boil down to scores on 57 different benchmarks.
@fig-panels below shows, for any pair of frontier or near-frontier models, how benchmark scores determine their ECIs.

## The math underlying the ECI {#sec-model}

Very briefly, an AI model's score on a given benchmark is [modeled](https://epoch.ai/data/eci-documentation/methodology) as a function of its capability $C_m$ (also referred to as its ECI):
$$
y_{mb} = S\big(\alpha_b \, [C_m - D_b]\big)
$$ {#eq-eci}

where $D_b$ is the benchmark difficulty, $\alpha_b$ the benchmark slope, and $S(x) = 1 / (1 + e^{-x})$ is the S-shaped logistic curve rising from 0 to 1. The scatter of the "noise" in each benchmark's scores is denoted $\sigma_b$. 
Epoch fits all the capabilities, difficulties, slopes, and $\sigma_b$'s at once as a [Bayesian model](https://epoch.ai/publications/eci-bayesian-methodology#what-changes-in-the-bayesian-model).

## Interpreting a model's ECI via its scores {#sec-panels}

Because all parameters are fit jointly, each benchmark score influences the difficulty, slope, and scatter parameters at the same time as it influences a model's ECI, $C_m$. As an approximation, though, we can use the estimated values of each benchmark's difficulty, slope, and $\sigma_b$ to assess the influence of a given benchmark score on $C_m$. Each individual benchmark score can then be thought of as a separate measurement of capability: a score $y_{mb}$ implies $\hat{C}_{mb}$, the capability at which that score is most likely.^[For mid-range scores this is close to inverting @eq-eci, $D_b + S^{-1}(y_{mb}) / \alpha_b$ with $S^{-1}(s) = \ln[s / (1 - s)]$. Toward 0% or 100% the two diverge, because the assumed noise there is lopsided: typically by a few ECI points beyond 5% or 95%, and by a dozen or more for scores of exactly 0% or 100% (which Epoch clips to 0.1% and 99.9%).] A model's ECI can be thought of as a weighted average of these measurements, $C_m \approx \sum_b w_{mb} \hat{C}_{mb} / \sum_b w_{mb}$, with weights that are, approximately,[^exact]

$$
w_{mb} \propto \alpha_b^2 \left[\left(\frac{1}{4\sigma_b^2} - 3\right) \mu_{mb} (1 - \mu_{mb}) + 1\right], \qquad \mu_{mb} = S\big(\alpha_b \, [C_m - D_b]\big)
$$ {#eq-weight}

A benchmark score counts most when the corresponding part of that benchmark's curve is steep and there is little noise. A score near the top or bottom ends of a benchmark counts for less, and one anywhere on a benchmark whose estimated curve is flat counts for little. Otherwise, the scatter $\sigma_b$ of benchmark noise runs from 0\.040 (METR Time Horizons) to 0\.179 (MirrorCode), so, other things equal, a mid-curve score on the former counts about 20 times as much as one on the latter.

[^exact]: @eq-weight approximates the information a score carries about $C_m$ under Epoch's likelihood, $[\alpha_b \mu_{mb} (1 - \mu_{mb})]^2 \kappa_b^2 [\psi'(\mu_{mb} \kappa_b) + \psi'((1 - \mu_{mb}) \kappa_b)]$ with $\kappa_b = 1 / (4 \sigma_b^2) - 1$ and $\psi'$ the [trigamma function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trigamma_function). The weight shares shown in @fig-panels are exact: each is the score's pull on $C_m$ divided by its distance $\hat{C}_{mb} - C_m$, which makes the weighted average the capability that best fits the model's scores given every benchmark's fitted curve and scatter; the expression above is their value for a score whose implied ECI is close to $C_m$. Not included in the calculations depicted in @fig-panels is the way Epoch's ECI also averages over uncertainty in the benchmark curves, so the two differ slightly: by 0\.03 points for the median model at ECI 150 and above, and at most 0\.32. (Epoch does not publish $\sigma_b$ updates, so the values here come from a refit of Epoch's [published model code](https://github.com/epoch-research/eci-public), which reproduces its ECI to within sampling error.)

(The chart below can optionally display analogous measures for an alternative ECI fit, XCI v0.1 [<i class="bi bi-github"></i>](https://github.com/hugetim/xci){title="XCI v0.1 code" aria-label="XCI v0.1 code on GitHub"}. It is the [XB+ variant](https://hugetim.github.io/posts/eci-clip/#xb-median-centered-beta-with-capability-scale-noise-also) with the capability noise spread allowed to vary by benchmark, $\tau_b$.)

